    h2('Net benefit at stake (E11; Supplemental Table S35 and main Figure 4)')
    p(f"Protocol extensions5_protocol_20261010.md was saved before computing (pre-addendum SHA-256 {X5['protocol_sha256_before_addendum'].upper()[:8]}…). For a risk-threshold rule with threshold p, treating a person whose usual-level risk is r has net benefit (r − p)/(1 − p) relative to not treating, in net true-positive units over the risk horizon (decision-curve logic with the threshold as the benefit–harm exchange rate). The regret of a single-examination decision is |r − p|/(1 − p) when it differs from the usual-level decision; the loss fraction is the expected regret divided by the achievable net benefit, E[max(r − p, 0)]/(1 − p), the net benefit of deciding on the usual level relative to treating no one. The relative risk reduction cancels in the fraction. The measurement model is that of the usual-level analysis (E4): the linear predictor at each examination equals the usual level plus half the systematic between-examination difference (sign by examination) plus an error of SD σ estimated from the two-examination differences; the usual-level distribution is the nonparametric maximum likelihood estimate on a 400-point grid, fitted to the two-examination means. Expectations average over the two examinations. Three strategies were compared: one examination; the average of two days (error SD σ/√2); and a second day only when the first estimate lies in the threshold's ≥25% zone (Monte Carlo, 200,000 draws). Thresholds and populations: base PREVENT 10-year ASCVD at 5% (primary), 3% and 10% in statin-naive participants (n = {X5['ascvd10']['n']}); 10-year total CVD at 7.5% in participants not taking antihypertensive medication (n = {X5['cvd10_bp']['n']}; all blood pressure levels); and 30-year ASCVD at 10% at ages 30–59 years (n = {X5['ascvd30']['n']}). Prespecified readings of the primary loss fraction were <5% minor, 5–<15% modest and ≥15% substantial, with an interval spanning two readings reported as such. Sensitivity analyses: empirical symmetrized errors; a smooth deconvolution in place of the NPMLE; a model-free version with the two-examination mean as the usual level (which understates regret); and, after the audit, error SD by age band, error variance inflated 1.5- and 2-fold relative to a longer-term usual level (the extra variance shared by both examinations), the 5% threshold within ages 30–59 years, and the loss relative to the better of treating all and treating none.")
    p("Bootstrap intervals. The protocol specified the first 1,000 of the frozen 4,000 cluster-bootstrap replicates with each replicate's EM warm-started from the full-sample solution and limited to 400 iterations, as in E4. The independent audit of E11 found that warm starting narrows the intervals relative to refitting each replicate as the point estimate was fitted. The final run therefore refits every replicate from a uniform start with SQUAREM-accelerated EM (20,000 EM evaluations; the full-sample SQUAREM fit agreed with plain EM to <3 × 10⁻⁵ in the loss fraction), and both intervals are reported; the uniform-start interval is used for interpretation. The audit also found that the 30-year replicates had been generated from the subgroup's own cluster labels; the final run uses the frozen replicates restricted to the subgroup. All point estimates were unchanged. The same correction was applied to the κ interval of E4 " + (f"(third addendum to extensions2_protocol_20261010.md; {E4U['n_boot']} uniform-start refits; full-sample SQUAREM κ differed from plain EM by {E4U['abs_diff_kappa_squarem_vs_plain_em']:.1e}); the original and rerun intervals are both given in Supplemental Table S32 and the rerun interval in main Table 2." if E4U else '(rerun pending at the time of this build; main Table 2 shows the original interval).'))
